-- #1300, SECOND SHAPE: a `(..)` member whose name is a type AND a constructor.
--
-- The sibling fixture `ctor_scope_ctor_member_wild_suffix/` covers the case where
-- the `(..)` lookup MISSES.  This one covers the case where it HITS and the bare
-- name is ALSO a constructor, so the two halves must both fire.
-- `frontend/resolve.expandMemberNames` binds the member name and then the named
-- type's constructors:
--     Some ctors => (name, useMemberLocal m, loc) :: map (c => (c, c, loc)) ctors
-- `backend/private_mangle.ctorMemberEntry` bound only the second half, so `Vee` — a
-- constructor of `SA`, which the front end DID put in scope — never entered the
-- rename map.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `Vee 7` is `SA`'s constructor, so `unSA (Vee 7)` = `7`; `Wye 2` comes from
-- expanding the type `Vee`, so `unVee (Wye 2)` = `2`.  So `7` then `2`.
--
-- ⚠️ COUNTERFACTUAL, measured rather than asserted.  With
-- `compiler/backend/private_mangle.mdk` restored to bfee53b4 and the binary rebuilt,
-- this fixture is RED on the native arm:
--     $ medaka build entry.mdk -o e
--     error: emitter failed compiling entry.mdk
--     runtime error [E-PANIC]: unbound variable 'Vee' (not a local, ctor, or known fn)
--     exit 1
-- while `medaka run entry.mdk` prints `7` / `2` at exit 0.  Note WHICH name the old
-- binary names: `Vee`, the member name itself — the half the old code never bound —
-- and not `Wye`, which came from the expansion it did perform.
import smodw.{Vee(..), unSA, unVee}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString (unSA (Vee 7)))
  putStrLn (intToString (unVee (Wye 2)))
